WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.334
-Our next guest is
an Academy Award

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:03.367
and Grammy winner,

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:04.667
a Rock and Roll Hall of Fame
inductee,

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:06.801
and former frontman
of Talking Heads.

00:00:06.801 --> 00:00:09.334
His latest work,
"Here Lies Love,"

00:00:09.334 --> 00:00:11.767
is playing at the
Broadway Theatre on Broadway.

00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.334
Please welcome back to the show
our friend David Byrne.

00:00:14.334 --> 00:00:16.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:26.334
♪♪

00:00:26.334 --> 00:00:27.334
♪♪

00:00:27.334 --> 00:00:29.734
-Welcome back.
-Thank you!

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:30.934
-I'm so happy you're here.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:32.000
I'm glad to be here.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.000
-And I know every time
you're here,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.634
and it's the way you get
everywhere in New York City,

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:37.200
you biked here today.
-Yeah.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.501
It's locked outside.

00:00:38.501 --> 00:00:41.033
So, you, David Byrne,
bike to 30 Rock,

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:42.734
and then you just
locked it outside?

00:00:42.734 --> 00:00:44.501
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:44.501 --> 00:00:47.133
-And how recognizable
are you to people

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:48.934
as David Byrne on a bike?

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:51.634
-Most of the time, no,
but somehow they know

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:52.868
when I'm coming to do this show.
-Yes. Interesting.

00:00:52.868 --> 00:00:56.767
'Cause you wear a helmet,
obviously.

00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:57.801
You play it safe.
-Most of the time, yeah.

00:00:57.801 --> 00:01:00.067
-Okay. Most of the time.
-Yeah, most.

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:01.834
-Have you --
Is the biking a new thing,

00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:03.901
or have you always been
a New York City biker?

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:06.267
-I've been doing it
for a long time.

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:08.901
Used to live on the
Lower East Side back in the --

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:10.934
-Yeah.
-A long time ago.

00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:12.234
There were hardly
any cabs down there.

00:01:12.234 --> 00:01:13.501
-Gotcha.

00:01:13.501 --> 00:01:16.834
-Hardly any cabs in SoHo.
It was like this weird,

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:19.067
dangerous, whatever.
-So it was out of necessity

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:20.567
that you were a biker?
-Kind of, yeah.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:22.834
It became really practical.
-And it's become safer.

00:01:22.834 --> 00:01:24.634
I think New York City has gotten
a little bit better for bikers,

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:26.200
but not entirely safe.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.267
-Not entirely.
I wouldn't go to a friend

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:29.968
and say --
who's never done it and say,

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:31.868
"Oh, just go ahead,
just go ahead.

00:01:31.868 --> 00:01:33.067
Just jump in there."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:35.667
-Because you -- I know
when you went on tour,

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:39.000
you and your cast
would bike in other cities.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.400
-Yeah.
-So it must be nice.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.934
There must be some
American cities where you go,

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.200
"Oh, one day.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.334
Wouldn't it be great
if New York was like this?"

00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:48.801
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
I mean, there --

00:01:48.801 --> 00:01:51.968
Austin has -- amazingly
has incredible bike lanes.

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:55.567
Portland, of course,
of "Portlandia," yes.

00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:57.267
Lots of bike lanes.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:59.000
-They do.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.801
So, congratulations.
Your second Broadway show.

00:02:01.801 --> 00:02:03.200
-Yes.
Well, yes, it is.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.100
-As a guy who used to bike
around the Lower East Side,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.467
did you ever think you'd have
two Broadway shows?

00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:10.033
-Uh, br-br-br-br, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:12.868
-So, this is based on an album
you did with Fatboy Slim?

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:16.100
It is a --
It's about Imelda Marcos,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:20.167
the wife of the former dictator
in the Philippines.

00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:22.300
-Exactly.
-And what made you first think

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:27.067
that this would be
a good topic for -- of music?

00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:28.634
-Just cried out to me. Yes.

00:02:28.634 --> 00:02:33.133
No. I'd been to --
Way back in the day,

00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:36.267
I'd been to some discos,
but I usually just went to see

00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:39.400
if there was a musical act
performing.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.701
And the acts who had club hits
in those days

00:02:43.701 --> 00:02:47.067
would come into the disco,
stand on a little platform,

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:50.300
put in a tape that had their
backing tracks of their song,

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.434
and just sing their two hits,
and they'd be gone.

00:02:53.434 --> 00:02:56.067
And I thought,
"But what if they could do more?

00:02:56.067 --> 00:03:00.234
What if you could do
a whole evening

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:04.767
and tell a story that way
while the audience is dancing?"

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:07.467
I like the idea of, "What
if you can have an audience

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:08.968
that's only half
paying attention?"

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:12.133
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.133 --> 00:03:14.067
-Somehow that appealed to me.

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:18.033
And -- because I thought,
"They'll absorb it.

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:20.367
They'll absorb the story.
They don't have to be

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:23.267
just paying attention
every second."

00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:26.033
Put that in my back pocket.
And then years later,

00:03:26.033 --> 00:03:31.601
I read that Imelda Marcos
loved going to clubs.

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:35.200
She had a mirror ball installed
in her New York townhouse,

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.567
which we don't all have.

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:38.367
-No, we don't all have.
-No, not all of us.

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:40.267
-We all should, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:46.300
-So, I googled that up and saw
a video of her dancing

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:51.400
under her home mirror ball
with Khashoggi, the arms dealer.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.367
-Wow.
-Yeah, and I thought,

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:58.067
"Uh, she lives in this world
that I've been thinking about

00:03:58.067 --> 00:03:59.801
if there could be
a show that way.

00:03:59.801 --> 00:04:04.234
And look who she's dancing with.
There's a story here."

00:04:04.234 --> 00:04:06.434
-So -- So it just clicks
together like that.

00:04:06.434 --> 00:04:09.100
-Yeah. So I can bring
these two ideas together,

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:12.267
these two things together,
and let's see what happens.

00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:13.467
Let's see if there's a story.

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:14.734
-And again, so I'm going
to show a photo.

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:17.801
This is a Broadway theater
here in New York City.

00:04:17.801 --> 00:04:19.100
You've taken out all the seats

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.701
so that the audience
on the ground floor there,

00:04:21.701 --> 00:04:23.701
in the orchestra area,
just sort of dances

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:24.901
throughout
the course of the show.

00:04:24.901 --> 00:04:26.367
-Yes.
So, we actually --

00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:27.601
I mean, okay.

00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:29.701
If you don't want to dance,
you don't have to.

00:04:29.701 --> 00:04:31.234
There are seats.
-Yes.

00:04:31.234 --> 00:04:33.200
-There are seats.
-There's nice balcony seats,

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:34.667
so you don't have
to be with this...

00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:38.834
-But my dream came true,
and I could do a show

00:04:38.834 --> 00:04:41.100
where a good portion
of the audience is...

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.534
-Yeah.
-...able to dance.

00:04:42.534 --> 00:04:45.667
-It must make you so happy
to see that this idea you had

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:47.033
all those years ago
is paying off.

00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:48.567
And it's
an entirely Filipino cast.

00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:50.067
That must be an exciting thing
to put together.

00:04:50.067 --> 00:04:51.367
-That's very exciting.
They're kind of telling

00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:53.334
their own -- the story
of their own country.

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:54.734
-Uh-huh.
And do you feel like --

00:04:54.734 --> 00:04:56.367
I mean, obviously, people
who buy Broadway tickets,

00:04:56.367 --> 00:04:58.767
you know, they maybe aren't
expecting something like this.

00:04:58.767 --> 00:05:00.300
Do you feel like
you've prepared them enough

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.467
for what they're about
to walk in for?

00:05:02.467 --> 00:05:03.601
-I hope so.

00:05:03.601 --> 00:05:04.901
I hope when they go
to buy the tickets,

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:06.267
they read the thing that says

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:08.767
"If you buy a ticket
on the dance floor,

00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:12.234
you will be standing
for an hour and a half,

00:05:12.234 --> 00:05:14.334
but you're not
gonna miss anything."

00:05:14.334 --> 00:05:15.834
It's all happening
all around you.

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:22.133
But if you want a seat, there's
lots of ways to sit, as well.

00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:26.100
Yeah, I haven't --
I go about once a week.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.467
-That's exciting.

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:30.400
-And I usually go on the dance
floor because I just --

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.267
I feel like I'm immersed.
I'm in it, then.

00:05:32.267 --> 00:05:33.734
-Well, I feel like
you are someone

00:05:33.734 --> 00:05:36.567
who has always
seemed to be taken over by music

00:05:36.567 --> 00:05:38.267
and wanted to dance.

00:05:38.267 --> 00:05:40.300
-I always wanted to, yes.
-Yeah.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.734
That's why we --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:43.534
That's why we never play music
during the interviews.

00:05:43.534 --> 00:05:45.701
Because we're afraid
you'll just wander off, yeah.

00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:47.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.834
I always ask people like this,
especially ones

00:05:48.834 --> 00:05:50.534
who've had such long,
iconic careers.

00:05:50.534 --> 00:05:53.434
Do you ever hear when
you're at a cafe or in a car,

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:54.834
do you ever hear your music?

00:05:54.834 --> 00:05:57.701
And what is your reaction
when you hear songs of yours?

00:05:57.701 --> 00:05:59.968
-If I heard it,

00:05:59.968 --> 00:06:02.200
sometimes friends point it out.
I don't notice.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.701
-Really.
-I don't notice.

00:06:03.701 --> 00:06:05.400
I have it tuned out.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.601
It's like another part of my
brain or -- for musicians.

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.601
They start to they listen
to music

00:06:10.601 --> 00:06:12.868
in a different way.
And my friends will point --

00:06:12.868 --> 00:06:15.701
"David, David, David,
listen to that.

00:06:15.701 --> 00:06:18.667
Do you know who that is?"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:21.033
-This sounds like they're
giving you an Alzheimer's test.

00:06:21.033 --> 00:06:24.601
-Yeah!
[ Laughter ]

00:06:24.601 --> 00:06:27.467
-Now this --
We've talked about this before.

00:06:27.467 --> 00:06:28.868
For my money,
one of the best --

00:06:28.868 --> 00:06:30.868
the greatest
concert movie of all time.

00:06:30.868 --> 00:06:32.968
"Stop Making Sense"
was just re-released.

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:34.667
They were showing it
in theaters.

00:06:34.667 --> 00:06:35.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
Remastered.

00:06:35.901 --> 00:06:37.834
-Thank you!

00:06:37.834 --> 00:06:39.334
And you went.
You watched it.

00:06:39.334 --> 00:06:41.200
-Oh, I've watched it, yeah,
now, a few times

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.801
during the opening.
That was a couple weeks ago.

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:45.000
-How many years had it been
since you've seen it?

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.267
-It had been at least
10 years,

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:51.567
maybe 15, since I'd seen it
projected on a screen.

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:55.167
And when it got reprinted
from the mast--

00:06:55.167 --> 00:06:57.868
from the negative
and remixed and everything,

00:06:57.868 --> 00:07:01.734
I obviously went to
a screening, and I thought,

00:07:01.734 --> 00:07:03.868
"Wow, this looks great.
It sounds great,

00:07:03.868 --> 00:07:07.267
but who is that guy?"
[ Laughter ]

00:07:07.267 --> 00:07:10.167
He's a very serious young man.

00:07:10.167 --> 00:07:11.234
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.234 --> 00:07:15.200
He's very intent.
Is he having any fun?

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:16.434
And --
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:16.434 --> 00:07:17.968
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.968 --> 00:07:19.968
-It is -- I will say,
because obviously --

00:07:19.968 --> 00:07:23.067
I've met this era David Byrne
is who I met.

00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:24.734
And you were not
who I thought you'd be

00:07:24.734 --> 00:07:26.467
based on that era David Byrne,

00:07:26.467 --> 00:07:27.868
who seemed like
he had a sense of humor

00:07:27.868 --> 00:07:31.601
because there was something
so theatrical about the film

00:07:31.601 --> 00:07:34.133
and the performance that I --
You know, look, giant suit.

00:07:34.133 --> 00:07:35.834
That's really funny.
You know what I mean?

00:07:35.834 --> 00:07:37.100
-Yes.
-I feel like a serious guy

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.334
doesn't wear a giant suit

00:07:38.334 --> 00:07:40.834
unless he knows there's
something funny about it.

00:07:40.834 --> 00:07:42.067
But were you having fun?

00:07:42.067 --> 00:07:43.767
Like, when you -- I mean,
I know it doesn't look --

00:07:43.767 --> 00:07:46.033
But was your memory of it
before you saw it, do you think,

00:07:46.033 --> 00:07:48.501
"Oh, my God,
that was so much fun to do"?

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:51.167
-Yes. At the end --

00:07:51.167 --> 00:07:52.434
As the show progresses,

00:07:52.434 --> 00:07:54.267
I do loosen up,
and I'm having fun,

00:07:54.267 --> 00:07:57.100
and the music is just --
I'm just carried away by it.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.868
So, yes,
I am having fun, but...

00:07:59.868 --> 00:08:02.667
I'm also,
like a lot of performers,

00:08:02.667 --> 00:08:04.400
I'm thinking, "Okay.
What do I do next?

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.601
What happens next?

00:08:05.601 --> 00:08:08.667
Where am I supposed to be?
What's going on?"

00:08:08.667 --> 00:08:10.501
Yeah, there's a little bit
of that going on.

00:08:10.501 --> 00:08:12.701
-And was it different -- or, was
there a heightened sense of that

00:08:12.701 --> 00:08:14.667
because you knew, "This time
we're putting it on film"?

00:08:14.667 --> 00:08:16.300
Obviously, you've performed
those songs before,

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:19.367
but did you know the whole time,
"There's cameras here.

00:08:19.367 --> 00:08:21.067
This is the one for..."?

00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:23.133
-[ Inhales sharply ]

00:08:23.133 --> 00:08:24.701
Uh...
yeah, but then, after a while,

00:08:24.701 --> 00:08:25.934
you have to just
kind of go with it.

00:08:25.934 --> 00:08:28.300
You just have to go,
like, "We're doing a show.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:31.133
I'm playing to the audience,
not to the cameras."

00:08:31.133 --> 00:08:34.067
Yeah.
-We did an episode based on this

00:08:34.067 --> 00:08:35.234
starring Fred Armisen.

00:08:35.234 --> 00:08:37.501
Fred wrote it,
called "Final Transmission."

00:08:37.501 --> 00:08:39.601
And did you know --
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:39.601 --> 00:08:41.834
-So here's you guys.

00:08:41.834 --> 00:08:45.234
And, uh -- and then
here's Fred and Bill Hader.

00:08:45.234 --> 00:08:47.868
And there's Maya Rudolph.
There's Bill Hader.

00:08:47.868 --> 00:08:50.434
Did you know Fred
had a David Byrne impression?

00:08:50.434 --> 00:08:52.467
-Yes, I did.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:52.467 --> 00:08:55.100
But I --
I found out by accident.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.868
I went to a fairly small club
downtown

00:08:57.868 --> 00:09:01.267
to see a musical act
at one point,

00:09:01.267 --> 00:09:05.701
and Fred was the opening act.
And I'm there in the club,

00:09:05.701 --> 00:09:08.901
and I'm watching him do me.
And I --

00:09:08.901 --> 00:09:11.000
-By the way, so doing comedy
before a music show

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.367
is one of the hardest
things in the world.

00:09:12.367 --> 00:09:14.033
The only person I've ever seen
do it well is Fred.

00:09:14.033 --> 00:09:15.901
-Fred can do it.
-Because Fred does --

00:09:15.901 --> 00:09:17.234
He is a musician, as well.
-Yeah.

00:09:17.234 --> 00:09:20.400
-And so Fred does exactly
what a music crowd wants to see.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.067
Because a comedy --
if you went to a comedy club

00:09:22.067 --> 00:09:23.367
and did a David Byrne
impression,

00:09:23.367 --> 00:09:25.400
it might be a crazy bad idea.

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:26.968
But you do a David Byrne
impression

00:09:26.968 --> 00:09:28.501
at a comedy s--
at a rock show,

00:09:28.501 --> 00:09:29.634
it might actually work.

00:09:29.634 --> 00:09:30.801
But he didn't know
you were there?

00:09:30.801 --> 00:09:32.434
-He did other things, too,

00:09:32.434 --> 00:09:35.200
but he didn't know I was there.
I'm watching.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.400
I'm thinking, "He's doing me."
[ Laughter ]

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:39.968
"And he's good at it."

00:09:39.968 --> 00:09:42.067
[ Laughter ]

00:09:42.067 --> 00:09:44.367
After the thing --
He didn't know I was there.

00:09:44.367 --> 00:09:45.834
-Yeah.
-He came out.

00:09:45.834 --> 00:09:46.934
I'm still in the audience.

00:09:46.934 --> 00:09:49.167
He goes,
"David, I'm really sorry."

00:09:49.167 --> 00:09:51.234
[ Laughter ]

00:09:51.234 --> 00:09:54.100
"I hope that didn't bother you
too much."

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.033
No, I just said --
-You should have told him,

00:09:56.033 --> 00:09:57.767
like, one of my friends
called me and told me

00:09:57.767 --> 00:09:59.434
you were doing it,
and I came down..."

00:09:59.434 --> 00:10:02.767
[ Laughter ]
"...to confront you."

00:10:02.767 --> 00:10:04.367
Well, Fred is not alone.

00:10:04.367 --> 00:10:06.300
We're all such huge fans
of yours.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.501
It was so cool to pay homage
to that wonderful film,

00:10:08.501 --> 00:10:09.834
and I'm so glad people
could see it again.

00:10:09.834 --> 00:10:11.467
And it's just so lovely
to have you here.

00:10:11.467 --> 00:10:13.968
Two Broadway shows.
You're finally making good.

00:10:13.968 --> 00:10:15.934
You're finally making good.

00:10:15.934 --> 00:10:16.968
David Byrne, everybody.

00:10:16.968 --> 00:10:19.501
"Here Lies Love" is exceptional.

00:10:19.501 --> 00:10:20.767
It's playing
at the Broadway Theatre.

00:10:20.767 --> 00:10:22.634
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

